WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.635
-Our first guest tonight
is a Grammy-nominated,

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:04.704
Emmy-winning writer,
comedian, and actress.

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:06.272
You can see her
next in "Maestro,"

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:08.374
which is
in select theaters now

00:00:08.374 --> 00:00:10.109
and streams
on Netflix December 20th.

00:00:10.109 --> 00:00:11.110
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.645
-Have you met the gang?

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:14.480
Betty and Adolph.
They're just a riot.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:15.715
-Hello.
-Hello.

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:17.483
-Ellen. I'm sure
you've run into her

00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:19.118
in mother's studio, no doubt.

00:00:19.118 --> 00:00:21.453
-Hello, Felicia.
-Who did I miss?

00:00:21.453 --> 00:00:23.089
Have I missed anyone?

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:24.757
-Just the piano player.
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:24.757 --> 00:00:27.526
-Well, I figured you needed
no introduction.

00:00:27.526 --> 00:00:29.295
-Hello.
I'm Lenny.

00:00:29.295 --> 00:00:30.663
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:32.865
our friend Sarah Silverman,
everybody!

00:00:32.865 --> 00:00:34.867
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:49.448 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:49.448 --> 00:00:51.184
Welcome back.

00:00:51.184 --> 00:00:53.018
-This is so nice of you
to come out east.

00:00:53.018 --> 00:00:54.988
You're from your home in L.A.
right before the holidays.

00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:56.555
-Yeah, yeah.
Oh, God, yeah.

00:00:56.555 --> 00:01:01.093
I just spent a week with my,
um, common-law in-laws.

00:01:01.093 --> 00:01:02.495
-Okay.
So this is Rory.

00:01:02.495 --> 00:01:03.930
How long have you
and Rory been dating now

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.331
to have a common-law in-law?

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:06.933
-Well, I think it's supposed
to be 10 years,

00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:08.468
but we've been together
three years.

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:09.735
-Okay, gotcha.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:09.735 --> 00:01:10.836
That's enough.
So, common-law in-laws.

00:01:10.836 --> 00:01:12.071
Did you luck into it?

00:01:12.071 --> 00:01:13.606
Do you have a good pair
of common-law in-laws?

00:01:13.606 --> 00:01:15.974
-Oh, they're so great.
I love them so much.

00:01:15.974 --> 00:01:18.011
And my, uh -- his dad,

00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:21.614
Big Jim, um,
is a real raconteur.

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:23.282
-Okay, tells a story.
-So it's fun, yeah.

00:01:23.282 --> 00:01:24.483
Oh, he tells such stories.

00:01:24.483 --> 00:01:27.820
They're not --
They're not original stories.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:29.822
They're not -- you know,
he didn't make them up,

00:01:29.822 --> 00:01:32.458
but he just -- like --
Wow.

00:01:32.458 --> 00:01:34.460
I sat with him
right before I left,

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:39.165
and he told me the entire...

00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:44.070
scene-for-scene story of
the movie "Legends of the Fall."

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.405
-Oh, wow.
-Yeah.

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:47.139
I had never seen it.
-Yeah.

00:01:47.139 --> 00:01:48.741
-It's good!
-It is good.

00:01:48.741 --> 00:01:51.244
-A little long.
-It's a -- It is a long movie.

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:52.845
I would imagine,
without the visuals,

00:01:52.845 --> 00:01:54.714
it might be a little longer.
-Yeah, well...

00:01:54.714 --> 00:01:56.615
Theater of the mind,
like the Mummy Christmas.

00:01:56.615 --> 00:01:57.984
-Yeah, like,
Fred's always talking about

00:01:57.984 --> 00:01:59.719
theater of the mind.
-I like theater of the mind.

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:02.388
-Um, this is --
speaking of visual,

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:05.024
this is a beautiful film
that Bradley Cooper directed.

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:06.425
-Isn't it?
-It's gorgeous.

00:02:06.425 --> 00:02:07.727
-A feast for the senses.

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:09.595
-It is a feast for the senses.

00:02:09.595 --> 00:02:12.765
This is his film, "Maestro,"
about Leonard Bernstein.

00:02:12.765 --> 00:02:14.433
You play his sister.
-Uh-huh.

00:02:14.433 --> 00:02:16.635
-And you're wonderful in it.
-Thank you.

00:02:16.635 --> 00:02:18.204
Oh, thank you.
-Did you love you --

00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:20.005
-Did you love...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:20.005 --> 00:02:21.707
...period costumes?

00:02:21.707 --> 00:02:23.542
-Um. Oh, yeah.
It's really --

00:02:23.542 --> 00:02:25.144
It does all the work for you.

00:02:25.144 --> 00:02:28.480
You got a costume, and they make
your hair old-timey,

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.550
and they put stuff on you,
and you get a cigarette,

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:33.652
and then --
And the dialect coach

00:02:33.652 --> 00:02:35.788
tells you how to talk, and then
you just say the words.

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:37.289
-And then it looks great.
It looks great.

00:02:37.289 --> 00:02:39.358
-Yeah.
-How well did you know --

00:02:39.358 --> 00:02:42.194
Did you know Bradley
before this?

00:02:42.194 --> 00:02:45.998
-Yes. You know, it's --
That's such a trick --

00:02:45.998 --> 00:02:50.069
I did an interview with,
like, a newspaper, uh, lady.

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:52.071
You know,
"Hey, what's the scoop?"

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:53.873
No.
-Yeah, we all know

00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:57.276
how newspaper ladies talk.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:57.276 --> 00:02:58.978
-Person.
-Yeah.

00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:00.746
-Newspaper person.
-That's what it's called.

00:03:00.746 --> 00:03:03.650
-Yeah.
-Um, and, uh...

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.317
she's asking me about "Maestro,"

00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:07.853
and she said, "Did you --
How well do you know Bradley?"

00:03:07.853 --> 00:03:09.255
and I said, uh --

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:10.389
You know,
I didn't want to be like,

00:03:10.389 --> 00:03:12.358
"Oh, we're buddies."
You know, I don't --

00:03:12.358 --> 00:03:14.260
You know how it's not --

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:18.164
You know, I go,
"Uh, I know him.

00:03:18.164 --> 00:03:21.934
I've known him peripherally
for a long time, you know?

00:03:21.934 --> 00:03:23.336
And we're not buddies.

00:03:23.336 --> 00:03:26.471
But, you know, we've known
each other a bit here and there,

00:03:26.471 --> 00:03:28.107
but, you know, not --"

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:30.309
And then I did, like --

00:03:30.309 --> 00:03:32.845
We've been doing these
Q&amp;A's after screenings,

00:03:32.845 --> 00:03:35.982
and I was backstage
like a week later

00:03:35.982 --> 00:03:39.451
with Bradley and Carrie and

00:03:39.451 --> 00:03:44.290
and Matt Bomer,
and Bradley goes,

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:48.694
"You know, an interviewer
for the piece on you called me.

00:03:48.694 --> 00:03:50.763
She said that you said
we didn't know each other well.

00:03:50.763 --> 00:03:53.532
I was like,
'We're totally good friends.'"

00:03:53.532 --> 00:03:55.267
And I was like,
"That's so weird.

00:03:55.267 --> 00:03:57.169
Yeah, we're totally
good friends."

00:03:57.169 --> 00:03:59.271
And I'm just telling --
Now if he sees this,

00:03:59.271 --> 00:04:01.040
I'll be like, "I don't know.
I didn't want to be like,

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.343
'Yeah, we're good friends.'"
I mean, I don't know.

00:04:03.343 --> 00:04:06.378
I don't have his e-mail address.
But I have a cellphone number.

00:04:06.378 --> 00:04:08.114
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.114 --> 00:04:09.649
I feel like
that's a better friend.

00:04:09.649 --> 00:04:11.350
Like, if we --
Like, for example,

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:12.985
if you only
had someone's e-mail,

00:04:12.985 --> 00:04:16.189
I would say that person has told
you, "We're not friends."

00:04:16.189 --> 00:04:17.523
-Right.
-But I've only had

00:04:17.523 --> 00:04:19.892
his cellphone number
since the movie.

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:21.661
-Okay.
-Making the movie.

00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:24.063
But we -- you know,
I've seen him around.

00:04:24.063 --> 00:04:26.065
-Was the newspaper person
ever, like,

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:27.466
"Just give me
a straight answer, kid!"

00:04:27.466 --> 00:04:29.335
-Eh!
-"Do you know him or not?!"

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:30.603
[ Laughter ]

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:32.771
What do you mean 'peripherally
for a long time'?"

00:04:32.771 --> 00:04:34.974
-What is it to know someone?
-Oh, my God.

00:04:34.974 --> 00:04:36.843
-"Look, I'm trying --
I'm on deadline."

00:04:36.843 --> 00:04:38.477
[ Laughter ]

00:04:38.477 --> 00:04:39.812
I want to congratulate you,
as well.

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:40.946
-You have, like, the "press"
thing in your hat.

00:04:40.946 --> 00:04:42.281
-Of course.
That's how you know, yeah.

00:04:42.281 --> 00:04:43.583
That's the only way you know.
Otherwise, that's when

00:04:43.583 --> 00:04:44.650
like,
"Can I see your credentials?"

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:46.018
and they show you their hat.
-Like...

00:04:46.018 --> 00:04:47.286
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:47.286 --> 00:04:50.356
You had a wonderful special,

00:04:50.356 --> 00:04:53.926
and it was nominated for both
a Grammy and a Golden Globe.

00:04:53.926 --> 00:05:01.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:01.601 --> 00:05:03.703
"Someone You Love"
is wonderful.

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:05.137
-That's what it's called --
"Someone You love."

00:05:05.137 --> 00:05:07.573
-Yes. And this is, uh --

00:05:07.573 --> 00:05:08.775
You can see it on Max.
Correct?

00:05:08.775 --> 00:05:11.010
-That's right.
-And you filmed it --

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:12.278
You've done --
This is your fourth special.

00:05:12.278 --> 00:05:13.446
You've done your first three
in L.A.,

00:05:13.446 --> 00:05:14.681
which has been home
for a long time.

00:05:14.681 --> 00:05:16.181
But you went back
to the Wilbur Theatre,

00:05:16.181 --> 00:05:17.816
wonderful theater in Boston.
-Yes!

00:05:17.816 --> 00:05:18.884
-And we're New Englanders.

00:05:18.884 --> 00:05:20.586
Did you feel like
it was nice to do --

00:05:20.586 --> 00:05:22.421
I mean, did they feel like
a hometown crowd still to you?

00:05:22.421 --> 00:05:26.325
-"Hometown hero makes good!"
-Yeah.

00:05:26.325 --> 00:05:27.760
"Barely knows Bradley Cooper."

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.895
[ Laughter ]

00:05:29.895 --> 00:05:33.465
-I love playing the Wilbur.
It's always good there.

00:05:33.465 --> 00:05:37.737
And it's Boston, which feels
like the closest to home...

00:05:37.737 --> 00:05:38.938
-Yes, of course.

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:42.808
-...without actually going home.
-Right.

00:05:42.808 --> 00:05:45.211
It's a good like hour buffer
from where we grew up.

00:05:45.211 --> 00:05:47.513
-It's a 40-minute,
45-minute "buffah."

00:05:47.513 --> 00:05:49.715
-Yeah. It's a good to have
a little "buffah."

00:05:49.715 --> 00:05:51.784
[ Laughter ]
Speaking of Boston,

00:05:51.784 --> 00:05:54.687
you, obviously, have been
nominated for some awards here,

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:56.155
obviously for this.

00:05:56.155 --> 00:06:00.459
You won an Emmy
for music and lyrics.

00:06:00.459 --> 00:06:02.662
I didn't realize
that your Emmy is for

00:06:02.662 --> 00:06:04.230
"I'm [bleep] Matt Damon."
There you are.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:05.631
[ Laughter ]

00:06:05.631 --> 00:06:07.866
I mean, this is
a Boston collaboration.

00:06:07.866 --> 00:06:09.902
This is my --
This is my "Good Will Hunting,"

00:06:09.902 --> 00:06:11.904
this song.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:11.904 --> 00:06:13.206
Was that -- I mean,
that must have been so --

00:06:13.206 --> 00:06:14.440
I don't know.
It's a ways back now.

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:15.908
But it must have been
so fun to do.

00:06:15.908 --> 00:06:17.810
And the response to it --
I remember this was, like,

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:19.378
the first thing
that everybody was like --

00:06:19.378 --> 00:06:20.880
that I knew that everybody's
like, "You've got to watch that

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:22.581
on YouTube."
-Yeah, like, went "viral,"

00:06:22.581 --> 00:06:23.815
you know, back in the day.

00:06:23.815 --> 00:06:25.485
And I remember, after that,
people would like,

00:06:25.485 --> 00:06:27.019
want me to make videos for them.

00:06:27.019 --> 00:06:29.355
And they're like, "We'd like you
to make a viral video."

00:06:29.355 --> 00:06:31.524
And I was like, "Yeah,
everyone would like to make

00:06:31.524 --> 00:06:33.792
a viral video.
That's not your decision.

00:06:33.792 --> 00:06:35.194
Like, you have
no control over it."

00:06:35.194 --> 00:06:36.828
-I was like, "You know,
it's just a new word

00:06:36.828 --> 00:06:39.298
for 'popular,'"
like, when they used "viral."

00:06:39.298 --> 00:06:41.467
-"Could you make a smash hit?"
-Yeah.

00:06:41.467 --> 00:06:43.302
-"Sure, I'd love to."
-"I would love to.

00:06:43.302 --> 00:06:45.003
They just --
They don't always work."

00:06:45.003 --> 00:06:47.940
You've had a career
full of smash hits,

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.808
but I did not know...
-What are these pictures?

00:06:49.808 --> 00:06:52.278
This is one
of your first --

00:06:52.278 --> 00:06:53.946
Which "Star Trek" franchise --

00:06:53.946 --> 00:06:56.782
-I'm here promoting
"I'm [bleep] Matt Damon"

00:06:56.782 --> 00:06:58.717
from 2007...
-Yeah, and then...

00:06:58.717 --> 00:07:00.653
-...and, uh,
"Star Trek: Voyager" from 1997.

00:07:00.653 --> 00:07:03.856
-But all all of these things
are later than, you know,

00:07:03.856 --> 00:07:06.559
when Leonard Bernstein was born.
So these are all more recent.

00:07:06.559 --> 00:07:08.327
-Okay, good.
-But "Maestro" --

00:07:08.327 --> 00:07:10.262
do see "Maestro," but I also
want to talk about this.

00:07:10.262 --> 00:07:11.464
So, "I'm [bleep] Matt Damon" --

00:07:11.464 --> 00:07:13.666
Basically,
what you're saying is,

00:07:13.666 --> 00:07:15.868
Bernstein, Silverman,
basically the same.

00:07:15.868 --> 00:07:18.171
-I'm saying you're
a composer, as well, yeah.

00:07:18.171 --> 00:07:22.208
All right. But here you go.
This is "Star Trek: Voyager"?

00:07:22.208 --> 00:07:24.810
-Oh, first of all, I kiss him.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:26.179
I love that you have
this picture.

00:07:26.179 --> 00:07:27.379
I don't have my glasses on,

00:07:27.379 --> 00:07:29.515
but this does look like
the back of my ass.

00:07:29.515 --> 00:07:31.517
-Yeah.
-I kissed him

00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:33.052
at the end of the episode,

00:07:33.052 --> 00:07:35.187
and he was standing
on an apple box

00:07:35.187 --> 00:07:38.191
because God forbid you're
the same height as the girl.

00:07:38.191 --> 00:07:40.426
-Yeah.
-And then I made out with him

00:07:40.426 --> 00:07:41.827
in real life.
-Oh, wow!

00:07:41.827 --> 00:07:43.662
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:43.662 --> 00:07:44.931
-Can you tell us what job
you had

00:07:44.931 --> 00:07:46.965
on this episode
of "Star Trek: Voyager"?

00:07:46.965 --> 00:07:48.768
-I play a scientist.

00:07:48.768 --> 00:07:49.902
[ Laughter ]

00:07:49.902 --> 00:07:51.370
And I went for the fitting,
and I go --

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:53.038
They've got me in, like,
a half shirt.

00:07:53.038 --> 00:07:54.773
-Yeah.
-"Yeah, you're a scientist with,

00:07:54.773 --> 00:07:56.842
like, a push-up bra
and a half shirt."

00:07:56.842 --> 00:07:59.444
-Yeah. And it looks like
a very tiny backpack.

00:07:59.444 --> 00:08:01.380
Like, it feels like a scientist
would have a bigger backpack.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:04.116
-Right.
And then that's my VW van,

00:08:04.116 --> 00:08:05.751
which for a very long time,

00:08:05.751 --> 00:08:08.353
I played someone
who drove a VW van.

00:08:08.353 --> 00:08:10.623
I think three different things
I had this car.

00:08:10.623 --> 00:08:12.591
-Really? You probably
got really good at it.

00:08:12.591 --> 00:08:13.659
At that point,
they were like --

00:08:13.659 --> 00:08:15.161
Your agent was like,
"She can do it."

00:08:15.161 --> 00:08:18.097
-The stick is, like, a long
stick with a ball at the end.

00:08:18.097 --> 00:08:20.499
-It's not easy.
Not any any actor can do it,

00:08:20.499 --> 00:08:21.600
but Sarah can.
-That's right.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:23.068
-You're so wonderful
in this movie.

00:08:23.068 --> 00:08:24.803
It's really beautiful, and it's
always so great to see you.

00:08:24.803 --> 00:08:25.837
You guys,
that's Sarah Silverman.

00:08:25.837 --> 00:08:28.174
-I love you.
-"Maestro" is in

00:08:28.174 --> 00:08:30.643
select theaters now and streams
on Netflix December 20th.

00:08:30.643 --> 00:08:32.545
We'll be right back
with Greta Lee.

